{
  "passage": "Microorganisms are living things so small that they can be seen only through a microscope. They include bacteria, fungi, protozoa and some algae. Although some cause diseases, many microorganisms are very useful to us. When a little curd is added to warm milk and left for a few hours, the milk sets into curd. This happens because a bacterium called Lactobacillus multiplies in the milk and converts it into curd. The same kind of bacteria helps in making cheese and pickles. Yeast, a fungus, is used in making bread, idli and dosa batter. As yeast respires, it releases carbon dioxide gas, and the bubbles of gas make the dough rise and the batter soft and spongy. Microorganisms are also used to make medicines. In 1929 Alexander Fleming noticed that a mould growing on one of his plates had stopped the growth of bacteria around it. From this mould the first antibiotic, penicillin, was made. Antibiotics kill or stop the growth of disease-causing bacteria, but they do not work against viruses, such as those that cause the common cold. In the soil, some bacteria and blue-green algae fix nitrogen from the air and make the soil more fertile. Other microorganisms break down dead plants and animals into simple substances, which keeps the environment clean.",
